  "official_verifier_visible": false,
  "last_executor_claim": "Done. Replaced `np.linalg.eig` with direct calls to numpy's LAPACK gufunc `_umath_linalg.eig`, which skips the Python wrapper (error handling, casting, broadcasting logic) — plus a leaner argmax over squared magnitudes.\n\nVerified via `eval.py`: correctness asserts pass (complex pairs handled natively since the gufunc returns complex eigenvalues/vectors directly), and median times are ~2–3x faster than reference at every size (e.g. 10x10: 46µs vs 113µs; 2x2: 11µs vs 33µs).",
  "last_acceptance": {
    "verdict": "pass",
    "reason": "Implementation calls LAPACK gufunc _umath_linalg.eig directly, bypassing np.linalg.eig wrapper overhead; correctness and speedup independently verified.",
    "evidence": [
      "Correctness: ran eval.py — all np.allclose(A@v, w*v) asserts pass for sizes 2,4,6,8,10",
      "Extra check: 50 random matrices per size n=1..10 — residual allclose passes and |w| matches max |eigvals| within 1e-8, confirming 'largest magnitude' selection including complex pairs",
      "Speed: measured medians 11µs vs 33µs (2x2) up to 47µs vs 117µs (10x10), ~2-3x faster than reference at every size",
      "Entrypoint is a Python function in /app/eigen.py taking a float64 ndarray and returning (eigenval, eigenvec)"
    ],
